Decision for the maintainer: options and a recommendation, not decided here.

1. Version string of the Perl CLI (`--version`, usage header line `skilletor <ver>`).
   (a) The skilletor version it follows (package.json, 0.7.0 today), tied to package.json by a test.
   (b) The Perl dist's own $VERSION (perl/lib/Skilletor.pm: 0.002); the CLI parity cases then normalise the version per engine.
   Recommendation: (a). The starter downloads the binary of the plugin's version, so the binary must report that version. Whether the CPAN dist follows the plugin version too is a separate question.
   Provisional (reversible): the Perl CLI prints the package.json version through one constant, pinned by a test. Switching to (b) means changing that constant and adding a normalisation.

2. Build vehicle for the binaries.
   (a) The Author::GETTY `binary` keys plus the reusable `dzil-binary.yml@binary-v1`. Covers five platforms including windows-x86_64, SHA256SUMS, install.sh and install.ps1. Costs:
       - it builds from the CPAN tarball of a `dzil release`, so Skilletor (and Text::Nunjucks, k159) must be on CPAN;
       - it needs its own dist release and tag in this repo, beside the plugin's vX.Y.Z tags;
       - the binary carries the dist version.
   (b) This repo's own workflow after skill perl-par-binary-release (getty source). It is triggered by the plugin tag vX.Y.Z, builds from the checkout (no CPAN), and is versioned with the plugin. It needs a Windows leg (Strawberry Perl), because the skill covers Linux and macOS only.
   Recommendation: (b) now. Revisit (a) once Skilletor and Text::Nunjucks are on CPAN and the versions are one.

3. Platforms.
   Options: linux-x86_64, linux-arm64, macos-arm64, windows-x86_64; macos-x86_64 optional.
   Recommendation: the first four. Add macos-x86_64 only on request.

From k188: a binary built on Debian 13 (glibc 2.41 host) needs glibc 2.38 (POSIX.so, MIME/Base64.so, libperl.so.5.40); the house ceiling is 2.31. Building on bullseye instead links OpenSSL 1.1, which Debian 12+ and Ubuntu 22.04+ no longer ship, so an https url source would need libssl bundled (untested). Part of option 2/3: which build image per platform.

From k189 (Linux x86_64 leg run locally in perl:5.40-bullseye): glibc floor 2.29, but it links OpenSSL 1.1 (libssl.so.1.1/libcrypto.so.1.1), which Debian 12+ and Ubuntu 22.04+ do not ship and RHEL 9 only as compat-openssl11 - an https url source would fail there; no smoke proves TLS either way. Options: bundle libssl with pp -l, a newer build image (higher glibc floor), or Net::SSLeay against a static OpenSSL. Unknown: how Net::SSLeay links OpenSSL on macOS, and whether the Windows binary carries Strawberry's OpenSSL DLLs.

